A||N||7||fsrowsell||Brake problem on my 86SE||||1||
Z||000000||fsrowsell||04-18-2001||12:34 AM||||Here is the problem I'm having with my front brakes... When I apply the breaks, the bake pads do not retrack. The only way to retrack them is to put a C clamp on and do it manualy. This is what I have done to solve the problem so far.... I took the Calipers off and cleaned them up. Then I bought a Caliper kit, which was the piston seal and caliper boot. I cleaned up the piston and the inner walls. Then I put all back together. The kit did not incloude O rings for the mounting bolt holes. So I just made sure it was very clean and took out the rings and clean them up as will. After doing all I that I put everything back on the car and the end result was I was back at square one again. It made no differance to my problem. Any one have any ideas on what else could be causing my sticking problem??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||04-18-2001||02:14 AM||||Are you saying that they stay clamped or just in contact with the rotors? They don't retract per se. They just don't have any pressure on the rotors when released.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-18-2001||08:04 AM||||Terryk is right.  There is nothing to force the pistons to retract.  They simply relax and ride just next to the rotor so pedal travel is minimized when the brakes are applied again.  If the pads stay clamped to the rotor when the pedal is released then you might have failing brake hoses.  They will sometimes brake down on the inside and form sort of a check valve not letting brake fluid return when the brakes are released.<P>It is important that the caliper is free to center itself on the rotor by sliding on the mounting bolt o-rings.  I always remove the o-rings and clean and lube them and the bolts when I do brake service.  You'd be surprised how many calipers are stuck and only apply pressure to one side of the rotor because the slider bolts are siezed up in the o-rings.  This causes reduced braking and warped rotors.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||04-18-2001||09:24 AM||||If the pads are maintaining contact with the rotors making the difficult to turn or creating a heat ot overheating problem, replace both flexiable hoses. They are broken down inside and restricting fluid movement under next to zero pressure. (back flow)||||reg||1
Z||000004||fsrowsell||04-18-2001||08:10 PM||||Thanks all, I think it is the flex holes, because the calipers are very clean like new with all new seals and the O rings are good. The pads are new and when I apply the breaks it won't let go and is causing the rotors to heat up. On payday I will get a couple of new holes. Thanks again for your help <BR>Felix<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B>If the pads are maintaining contact with the rotors making the difficult to turn or creating a heat ot overheating problem, replace both flexiable hoses. They are broken down inside and restricting fluid movement under next to zero pressure. (back flow)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||3FastbackGTs||04-19-2001||02:04 AM||||  hone your caliper bores...||||reg||1
Z||000006||3FastbackGTs||04-19-2001||02:16 AM||||  I had the same problem with my 87GT..I went thru the usual and seemingly obvious causes and solutions..new master cylinder...checking for pedal travel and binding,making sure the master cylinder piston was returning competely..ect..the pistons would grab just great..but would not release, holding the discs nice and tight. the only way to release them was to use the "C" clamp to push the piston back.  Well after hours and hours of frustration I went and bought a new brake cylinder hone and honed the bores until the piston would slide in without binding ...I feel about 99.9% sure ths is going to be the solution to your brake woes.<p>[This message has been edited by 3FastbackGTs (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000007||My7Fieros||04-19-2001||11:14 AM||||3Fastbacks is right.....the piston gets stuck..hone it out and they'll work like new again! M7F||||reg||14
